# Compose reuse (clean UI)

Prefer shared building blocks over one-off layouts pasted into large feature files.

## Search before you build

Before writing a new button, field, dialog, row, badge, card or scrim:

1. Search `core/designsystem/component/` (`button/`, `input/`, `dialog/`, `row/`, `selection/`, `card/`, …).
2. Search `core/ui/` for error/load scaffolds.
3. Search the current `feature/<name>/` for an existing private composable or chrome helper (e.g. `PillButton`, dialog hosts).

Reuse what exists. Only invent a new primitive when nothing fits.

## Where code lives

| Kind of UI | Put it in |
|---|---|
| Used (or clearly needed) in 2+ features | `core/designsystem/component/<group>/` via `build-ui-component` skill; public API change → impact analysis |
| Feature-only, used in 2+ places in that feature | private/internal composable in a focused file under `feature/<name>/` |
| One screen, one use | private composable in that screen’s file — keep it short |

Never copy-paste the same `BasicTextField` / scrim / confirm-footer block across files. Extract once.

## File size and split

- Aim for **~250 lines** per Compose file; hard stop around **400**. If you exceed that, split before adding more UI.
- Screen shell stays in `XxxScreen.kt` / `XxxRoute`; move sections to siblings, e.g. `CheckoutTeam.kt`, `TeamInviteForm.kt`, `TeamDialogs.kt`.
- Keep pure state/helpers in non-Compose files (`TeamState.kt`, not inside the Screen).
- One public entry composable per file when practical; private helpers may share the file until it grows past the limit.

## Prefer existing primitives (examples)

- Buttons → `AppButton` / `AppIconButton` (or the feature’s shared chrome button if that is the established pattern).
- Text fields → `AppTextField`, `NumericField`, `SearchField`, …
- Confirm / destructive → `ConfirmationDialog` + `AppModal`.
- Rows / tags → `ListRow`, `SettingsRow`, `AppTag`, `StatusBadge`.

Do **not** reimplement modal scrims, labeled fields, or primary/secondary action rows inline when a shared component already covers them.

## Composition checklist (before finishing a UI change)

- [ ] No new duplicate of an existing design-system or feature helper
- [ ] New shared look/feel was extracted (or an existing component was extended) instead of forked
- [ ] Files stay under the size guideline, or were split in the same change
- [ ] Previews cover the new extracted composable’s variants
- [ ] `scripts/ai-hooks/design-lint-check.sh` is clean on touched paths
